{"t":1788821404,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"54fc028e-c92b-4c8d-922c-4d94bd3776fb","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-07T19:09:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 071909\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n309 PM EDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNo significant changes have been made to the forecast for this\nlatest cycle.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Dry through mid week.\n\n- 2) Shower and thunderstorm chances return Thursday.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry through mid week.\n\nHigh pressure will persist over the Mid-Atlantic through\nWednesday before gradually moving offshore. Large scale\nsubsidence and dry air lead to a precipitation-free forecast\nwith temperatures rising to several degrees above normal as\nthe high pulls offshore. This leads to high temperatures in the\nmid to upper 80s Tuesday, perhaps near 90 Wednesday (slightly\ncooler in the mountains). Low temperatures (and dew points) \nwill generally be in the 50s and 60s during this time. Some\nriver valley fog is likely each morning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Shower and thunderstorm chances return Thursday.\n\nThe upper-level ridge will begin to flatten out Wednesday before \nbreaking down and pushing south and east into Thursday. Several \npieces of upper-level energy rotating around an upper low \ncentered over the Hudson Bay will slowly push a cold front south\ntowards our region by Thursday. At the surface, high pressure \npushing offshore will bring increasingly warm and humid \nconditions to the area, with highs in the 80s to near 90 with \ndew points in the upper 60s to low 70s, with the highest values \nbeing Thursday.\n\nModel guidance over the past 24 hours has come into better agreement \nin regards to the progression of the front on Thursday, as well as \nthe upper-level pattern in association with it. The front \nreally looks like it will flatten out as it moves southward, and\nreally loses the upper-level support. The timing of peak \nheating and the highest shear just doesn't look to line up at \nthis point. There may be some jet-induced showers and storms \nlate Thursday as we move into the right entrance of the upper \njet centered over New England, but not necessarily expecting \nthose to be severe at that point, as the front should be largely\nthrough the region. At this point, the severe weather threat \nlooks very limited, but any storm that develops on the warm side\nof the boundary could bring some gusty winds along with it, but\nthe lack of a substantial wind field will really make that more\ndifficult with southward extent.\n\nAs this cold front transitions offshore Friday, temps drop again\nand conditions become much drier heading into the start of the \nweekend as high pressure moves in overhead. The next system may\napproach by late next weekend.\n\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /18Z MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nVFR conditions are expected to prevail through the end of the\nweek, with two exceptions. Early morning river valley fog could\naffect mainly KCHO/KMRB each day roughly 08z-13z. Then, some\nshower and thunderstorm activity could bring brief restrictions\non Thursday (mainly in the afternoon and/or evening). Light NE\nwinds today become E to S tonight into Tuesday, then remain S\nthrough Thursday until a cold front brings a shift to W/NW.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nHigh pressure will result in light northeast winds becoming\nsouth through Tuesday. Winds may increase briefly early\nWednesday morning as the high shifts offshore, but are more\nlikely to increase in southerly channeling Wednesday afternoon\ninto Wednesday night. Winds shift to the west and northwest and\ncould remain gusty Thursday as a cold front crosses, possibly\nbringing a few showers and thunderstorms. Winds should become\nlighter heading into Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nTide levels remain around one foot above normal. With light\nnortheast winds becoming southerly, tide levels are likely to\nremain relatively steady around one foot above astronomical\nnorms through the middle of the week. The higher of the two\ndiurnal tides is in the later night/early morning period, during\nwhich time Annapolis is most likely to hit minor flood stage\nthrough early Thursday prior to a cold front shifting winds to\nthe west. Other sites like Havre de Grace, Bowley's Quarters,\nand Alexandria could approach minor flood at times, as well.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Coastal Flood Advisory from 11 PM this evening to 7 AM EDT \n     Tuesday for MDZ014.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...DHOF/CJL\nAVIATION...DHOF/CJL\nMARINE...DHOF/CJL\n"},"forecast":{"t":1788821383,"days":{"2026-09-07":{"hi":null,"lo":62,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Clear"},"2026-09-08":{"hi":88,"lo":66,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-09":{"hi":89,"lo":72,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-10":{"hi":90,"lo":70,"pop":49,"short":"Mostly Sunny then Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-11":{"hi":83,"lo":67,"pop":13,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-09-12":{"hi":84,"lo":69,"pop":29,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-13":{"hi":86,"lo":69,"pop":17,"short":"Slight Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-14":{"hi":86,"lo":null,"pop":8,"short":"Mostly Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1788818400,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0],"spd":[4,3,3,3,3,3,2,2,3,2,2,3,3,3,3,3,3,4,4,4,5,5,6,7,6,6